## Recent progress in MOFs-based nanozymes for biosensing
Imamdin Chandio, Yongjian Ai $(\boxtimes)$ , Lei Wu, and Qionglin Liang $(\boxtimes)$
MOE Key Laboratory of Bioorganic Phosphorus Chemistry & Chemical Biology, Department of Chemistry, Laboratory of Flexible Electronics Technology, Center for Synthetic and Systems Biology, Tsinghua University-Peking University Joint Centre for Life Sciences, Tsinghua University, Beijing 100084, China
$\circledcirc$ Tsinghua University Press 2023   
Received: 22 February 2023 / Revised: 11 April 2023 / Accepted: 23 April 2023
## ABSTRACT
Nanozymes  are  nanomaterials  with  enzyme-mimicking  catalytic  activity.  Compared  to  natural  enzymes,  nanozymes  show various properties such as easy to manufacture, stable, adjustable, and inexpensive. Nanozymes play key roles in biosensing, biocatalysis, and disease treatment. As an important kind of nanozymes, metal-organic framework (MOF)-based nanozymes are receiving  a  lot  of  attention  due  to  their  structural  properties  and  composition.  Rationally  developing  MOF  with  enzymes-like catalytic  properties  has  opened  new  perspectives  in  biosensing.  This  review  summarizes  the  up-to-date  developments  in synthesizing  two-dimensional  and  three-dimensional  MOF-based  nanozymes  and  their  applications  in  biosensing.  Firstly, classification of nanozymes obtained by MOFs is categorized, and different properties of MOF-based nanozymes are described. Then, the distinctive applications of MOF-based nanozymes in identifying various analytes are thoroughly summarized. Finally, the recent challenges and progressive directions in this area are highlighted.
## KEYWORDS
metal-organic frameworks, nanozymes, metal-organic framework (MOF)-based nanozymes, biosensing, analytes
## Introduction
In the modern era, the researchers have focused on sustainable, fast,  easy,  selective,  and  sensitive  analysis  tool  to  determine targeted  substances.  Many  biomacromolecules  and  small molecular weight organic substances have been analyzed because these  are  generally  used  in  daily  survival  and  have  negative impacts on the human health [1]. These substances involve food ingredients,  pharmaceuticals  utilized  in  animal  and  human medication,  and  occasionally  waste  or  byproducts  from commercial  and  industrial  operations.  Compounds  that  are hazardous,  carcinogenic,  or  otherwise  detrimental  to  living creatures  have  significantly  increased.  Therefore,  to  reduce  the influence of these substances at various levels, relevant agencies are constantly  working  to  propose  new,  stringent  policies  [2, 3].  In this  regard,  applications  of  biosensors  for  real-time  analysis molecules  in  various  types  of  samples  guarantee  that  legal requirements are fulfilled and that laws are followed [4]. Several substances are utilized to synthesize the biosensor, such as carbon derivatives,  quantum  dots  (QDs)  [5],  graphene  [6, 7],  fullerenes [8],  nanotubes  [9],  metal  colloids  [10],  and  silica  nanoparticles (NPs)  [11].  Nanomaterials,  such  as  metal-organic  frameworks (MOFs) [12] and molecularly imprinted polymers (MIPs) [13], or indeed  mixtures  of  these  molecules,  are  used  to  fabricate biosensors [14].
Biosensor  is  an  analytical  device  that  transfers  biological processes  into  detectable  signals,  such  as  the  electrical  signal related  to  substance  quantity  [15].  Biosensors  for  measuring analytes take advantages of simplicity, reusability, rapid response, high  specificity,  and  affordability  [16].  Biosensors  are  used  in several industries, including medical and health care, pharmaceutical,  analytical  quantity  of  pantothenic  acid,  biotin, B12, vitamin, and folic acid [17], and pollutants [18]. Biosensors provide  many  benefits  compared  to  other  investigation techniques,  such  as  high  sensitivity.  Biosensors  have  been integrated  into  various  machinery  and  equipment  used  in  food production  because  of  their  miniaturization  [19].  Research  on affinity-based  biosensors  and  related  technologies  is  developing rapidly.  Due  to  various  biosensor  approaches  that  have  been reported,  scientists  can  better  understand  the  kinetics,  structure, and (solid/liquid) interface phenomena connected to protein–ligand binding interactions.
Enzymes  are  naturally  accruing  molecules  used  as  effective catalysts  in  various  applications.  The  enzymes  are  composed  of proteins  and  are  used  in  many  fields,  such  as  medicine, bioengineering,  industry,  and  food,  owing  to  their  excellent substance specificity and outstanding catalytic performance. The primary difference between enzymes and synthetic catalysts is that chemical  reactions  accelerated  by  inorganic  catalysts  can  usually be  carried  out  at  high  pressures  or  temperatures.  In  contrast, enzymes  significantly  initiate  biological  responses  under  normal conditions of temperature and $\mathrm{\pH}$ [20]. Several enzyme mimics have  been  developed  and  investigated  to  overcome  the  intrinsic drawbacks  of  natural  enzymes,  including  high  manufacturing costs,  limited  stability,  and  storage  and  recycling  challenges.  In recent  decades,  nanomaterials,  such  as  rare  earth  nanoparticles, magnetic  nanoparticles,  and  gold  nanoparticles  (Au  NPs),  with outstanding  enzyme-like  activity  have  been  referred  to  as nanozymes  [21].  Due  to  the  rapid  advancement  of  nanoscience and  nanotechnology,  many  papers  have  been  published  on  indepth research on nanozymes and their fabrication [22].
Nanozymes are nanomaterials with enzyme-like characteristics [23, 24].  Nanozymes  are  artificial  enzymes  that  obey  enzyme kinetics (Mie’s equation) and enzymatic mechanism (ordered cis, stochastic  cis,  or  ping-pong  reactions).  Various  nanomaterials were  demonstrated  to  have  several  enzymatic  activities simultaneously. Some literature has reported that nanozyme has superoxide dismutase-like (SOD), hydrolase-like, catalase oxidaselike,  and  hydrogen  peroxidase-like  (POD)  activities.  Researchers have  discovered  that  compared  to  natural  enzymes,  nanozymes offer certain promising advantages, such as minimal cost, ease of synthesis, high stability, and customizable structure. Particularly in the analytical field, the flexible adjustment of nanozymes’ activity offers  rich  principles  and  methods  for  analyte  detection,  and nanozyme catalysis provides amplified signals for high-sensitivity quantitative  determination,  making  nanozymes  emerging  and promising materials for biosensing.
MOFs,  generally  referred  to  as  highly  porous  coordination polymers  (PCP),  are  polymers  that  may  self-assemble  by coordinating metallic ions with organic ligands [25, 26]. MOFs are new,  highly  organized,  and  porous  hybrid  inorganic–organic crystalline  materials  [27–29].  MOFs  have  several  distinguishing characteristics,  including  high  surface  area,  chemical  diversity, exceptional  structural  stability,  and  tailored  ultra-high  porosity [30–32]. As a result, they have demonstrated a broad range of uses in  purification,  chemical  catalysis,  nonlinear  optics,  sensing,  and drug  delivery.  Particularly  in  the  field  of  catalysis,  MOF-based nanozymes  have  attracted  the  attention  of  scientists  in  the  past years. MOF-based nanozymes have the advantages of specificity of the  pore  size  and  structure,  high  density,  perfect  dispersion  of active  sites,  and  greater  porosity  to  guarantee  the  availability  of catalytically  active  cores.  Due  to  the  diversification  of  functional groups of ligands, broad pores of nanochannels enhance substrate and  transfer  product  during  catalysis.  The  most  typical  MOFs nanomaterials  are  reported  to  be  octahedral,  dodecahedral,  and three-dimensional  (3D)  nanostructures,  which  are  commonly known as 3D MOFs [33].
Furthermore, two-dimensional MOF nanosheets (2D MOFNs), such as metal-sulfur compounds, boron nitride, black phosphorus, graphene,  and  graphitic  carbon  nitride,  have  progressively emerged in the public eye in recent years. 2D MOFs show strong enzymatic  activity  in  comparison  to  typical  3D  MOFs  [34].  2D MOFNs have been used in gas separation, catalysis storage, and sensing [35, 36]. Furthermore, the organic linkers on the benzene ring of MOFs may be functionalized with functional groups such as –OH or $\mathrm{-NH}_{2},$ sometimes even –COOH, to generate MOFsbased hybrids depending on requirements that can confer several selective  functionalities  to  the  appropriate  MOFs.  It  can  also  be employed as a carrier for active compounds, such as biocatalysts or  pharmaceuticals,  carrying  them  to  their  target  areas  and allowing them to function.
These MOFs’ enzyme-mimetic properties and versatility have significantly  improved  their  characteristics,  particularly  in biosensing. Herein, we summarize the up-to-date developments in the synthesis approach of 2D and 3D MOF-based nanozymes and their  applications  in  biosensing.  Firstly,  classification  of nanozymes  obtained  by  MOFs  is  categorized,  and  attractive characteristics  of  MOF-based  nanozymes  are  described.  The specific  applications  of  MOF-based  nanozymes  in  identifying various  analytes  are  thoroughly  studied.  Finally,  the  recent challenges and progressive directions are deliberated [37]. MOFs have  a  promising  future  due  to  their  versatility  for  MOF-based composite nanozymes in biosensing.
## 2    Different types of MOF-based nanozymes
The MOF primarily comprises two components, organic ligands for linking and positive ions (metals). The ligands act as a linker while  positive  metal  ions  act  as  nodes  of  framework  structure; hence  the  ligands’ structural  arrangements,  and  the  metals’ coordination  arrangement,  play  a  crucial  part  in  their  geometry and  structural  characteristics.  They  have  various  uses  as  bio enzymes  in  catalysis  due  to  their  consistently  adjustable  pores, high  specific  surface  area,  and  strong  biocompatibility. Furthermore, due to less thermal and chemical stability and poor electrical  conductivity,  the  MOFs  are  typically  coupled  with additional bioactive substances to improve hybrids by synergistic effects  [38, 39].  So  far,  MOFs  hybrid  nanostructures  have  been synthesized  using  different  active  NPs,  for  instance,  sulfides, polymers, metal or metal oxides, and ionic NPs. Based on the type of material, the MOF-based nanozymes are differentiated, which usually  comprise  the  preliminary  MOFs-structured,  and composite  nanozymes  synthesis  depends  on  MOFs.  Amongst them, pristine MOF nanostructural nanozymes consist of 3D and 2D  MOF  nanozymes.  These  MOF-based  nanozymes  are frequently used in colorimetric detection for sensing the amount of some constituents in biomedical samples through their essential enzymelike characteristics [40]. On the other hand, by choosing the  appropriate  material  to  integrate  with  MOFs  to  achieve  the desired  features,  MOF  composite  structured  nanozymes  for various  biological  demands  were  developed.  For  instance,  when used for bioanalysis, metal oxides and MOFs can also be coupled to  enhance  the  composite  material’s  complete  stability  and catalytic performance [41]. This part summarizes the state-of-theart developments in the synthesis approach of 2D and 3D MOFbased composite nanozymes.
In  the  case  of  pristine  MOFs,  the  organic  linkers  and  metals coexist. Hence, the following two factors may be the source of the enzyme-like catalytic ability of MOFs; for instance, the presence of these  metals,  MOFs  with  Ce,  Cu,  Fe,  Co,  or  Ni  ions  may  offer enzyme-mimicking  catalytic  performance.  On  the  contrary, certain unique aromatic compounds (ligands) in MOFs serve as electron mediators for donating electrons from one medium and accepting  electrons  from  another  medium  [42].  Several  MOFs have been investigated in recent decades as potential nanozymes with various enzyme functions, for instance, POD, oxidase, SOD, and  hydrolase  (Scheme  1).  Additionally,  it  has  been  discovered that  certain  MOFs  display  more  enzyme  activity  in  similar  or different environments.
## 2.1    Peroxidases
PODs  are  a  group  of  naturally  occurring  enzymes  that  may catalyze the hydrogen peroxide to a substrate with other reducing substrates.  Horseradish  peroxidase  (HRP),  a  common  POD,  is frequently used to catalyze $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ for various uses. In HRP, a big helical  protein  holds  active  hemin  as  a  reduction  and  oxidation reaction component. Numerous MOFs with Fe ions were shown POD-like catalytic activity. Property is just like HRP [21, 43–56]. Other metal-containing MOFs, such as Cu, Ru, Ni, and Fe-based, were  also  investigated  as  potential  POD  mimics  [57]. For biochemical  monitoring,  various  substrates  were  detected  by Raman,  chemiluminescent,  electrochemical,  colorimetric,  or fluorescent,  and  generated  signal.  For  example  tetramethyl benzidine  (TMB)  may  be  converted  towards  its respective  oxide oxTMB through POD mimic catalyzed $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ reaction [34, 44, 58, 59]. oxTMB initially exhibits blue before turning yellow after acid exposure, serving as the cornerstone for a colorimetric measuring device [60].
Additionally,  the  biphenyl  structure  of  the  oxTMB  molecule makes  it  simple  to  quench  other  fluorescent  labels,  making  it appropriate  for  fluorescence  detection  [61, 62].  Besides,  the oxTMB product may deliver distinctive Raman signals for surfaceenhanced  Raman  scattering  (SERS)  analysis  compared  to  the initial TMB [63]. Such modifications provide flexible identification techniques  for  bioassay.  However,  several  POD  mimics  were designed for various uses. Most of them show catalytic properties within  a  small $\mathrm{\pH}$ range.  Typical  POD  mimics  exhibit  greater characteristics  in  a  slightly  acidic $\mathrm{(pH=4)}$ ,  whereas  in  neutral media, they have very little or negligible enzymatic activity. Since many  biological  processes  should  occur  in  physiological conditions, this flaw restricts their potential application in bioassay [64]. POD mimic catalysis in a slightly acidic condition is used to measure  the  quantity  of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ generated  after  the  conversion  of glucose occurs in a neutral solution due to the action of glucose oxidase  (GOx).  Inconsistency  of  such  two  enzymes’ or  mimics’ operating  environments  hampers  the  sensing  process  and  may adversely  influence  the  efficiency  of  the  analysis.  Therefore,  it  is consistently  desirable  to  investigate  nanozymes  with  sensual activity  in  a  broad  range  of  pH  [33, 60, 65].  Recently,  He  and coworkers  anticipated $\mathrm{{Ru}(\mathrm{{III}})}$ based  metal-organic  gels  (RuMOGs) with promising POD-like activity in both basic and acidic mediums [66]. They discovered that the Ru-MOGs demonstrated good catalytic activity for the conversion of dopamine to amino chrome in a $\mathrm{pH}5.5$ buffer in the presence of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ (Figs. 1(a) and 1(b)),  as  well  as  the  potential  to  catalyze  the  luminol chemiluminescence  signal  in  basic  environment  (Figs.  1(c) and 1(d)). The biological detection method would benefit greatly from this broad pH application.
## 2.2 Oxidases
In contrast to POD, oxidases are another important class of MOFbased nanozymes. Usually, it is revealed that certain MOFs, $\mathrm{Cu},$ Co,  or $\mathrm{Ce}$ based,  demonstrate  oxidase-like  catalytic  properties. Without  the  support  of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2},$ they  may  independently  catalyze the oxidation of substrates. Conversely, these oxidase mimics the catalyzed processes that frequently included dissolved oxygen. Due to their easier reaction conditions, oxidase mimics are, in certain cases, superior to POD mimics for biological identification [59]. It is  well  acknowledged  that  the  catalytic  property  of  nanozymes greatly  depends  on  their  shape,  crystalline  structure,  size,  and surface  area.  In  addition  to  these  physicochemical  parameters, environmental  stimuli,  like  light,  can  also  affect  the  ability  of nanozymes  to  behave  as  an  enzyme.  Wei’s  team  published  a photosensitized  MOF  (PSMOF)  in  2019,  whose  light  exposure activated oxidase-mimetic action [67]. The $\left(\mathrm{Ru(bpy)}_{3}{}^{2+}\right)$ derivative was  mixed  with  Zn-based  MOFs  as  photoactive  ligands  to produce the PSMOF (Fig. 2(a)). The combination of the PSMOF with the TMB substrate did not result in a solution color change or  a  recognizable  absorption  when  there  was  no  light  exposure (Fig. 2(b)). The PSMOF quickly accelerated the oxidation of TMB when the solution was exposed to light, producing a blue color. Somebody  could  easily  control  the  enzymatic  process  with external power irradiation, which allows you to start and stop it. Figure 2(c) has important implications for developing customizable  biosensors  and  sensors.  One  might  read  the  most current evaluation from Wei’s team for further details regarding light-responsive  nanozymes  [68].  Specific  chemicals  and  light stimulation can activate some MOFs-based oxidase mimics　[69]. The oxidase-mimicking activity of MOFs $(\mathrm{CeO_{2}N R-M O F})$ with a ceria  nanorod  template  has  recently  been  demonstrated  to  be dramatically accelerated by $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ in a $\mathrm{{pH}=4}$ buffer [70]. Wang et  al.  discovered  that  the $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}\mathrm{NR}$ -MOF  catalyzed  the  TMB oxidation  process  quickly  (less  than $30s)$ in  the $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ environment.  Still,  the  chromogenic  process  proceeded  gently (greater  than $10\ \mathrm{min}.$ )  with  just  the ${\mathrm{CeO}}_{2}{\mathrm{NR-MOF}}$ (Fig. $2(\mathrm{d}))$ . $\operatorname{Au@Hg}$ and  polyethyleneimine-stabilized $\mathrm{Ag}$ nanoclusters  (PEIAgNCs) were used to identify photometrically hazardous $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ .
Nevertheless, there is controversy about the actual applications of  these  materials.  While  earlier  studies  [44, 71, 72]  suggested $\operatorname{Au@Hg}$ and  PEI-AgNCs  as  oxidoreductase  mimics,  Wang’s research  [70]  indicated  the ${\mathrm{CeO}}_{2}{\mathrm{NR-MOF}}$ worked  like  oxidase imitation.  In  addition,  the  reason  why $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ can  be  used  to increase the enzyme-like properties of these materials rather than other  particles  remains  a  mystery,  as  the  actual  mechanism  of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ -boosted  nanozyme  catalysis  is  also  unclear.  Laccase  is  a unique oxidase that may catalyze the conversion of phenols into benzoquinone  molecules.  Because  of  this  feature,  laccase  is frequently  used  to  identify  and  break  down  phenolic contaminants  and  is  influenced  by  real  laccase’s  physical characteristics  and  chemistry.  Liang  and  colleagues  created  a laccase mimic composed of copper and guanosine monophosphate  (GMP)  [73].  The  synthesized  Cu/GMP  MOF, which  had  a  Cu-to-GMP  ratio  of  3:4,  was  more  effective  than natural laccase at catalyzing the conversion of phenol-containing substrates  (Fig. 2(e)).  They  also  developed  an  epinephrine colorimetric  analysis  using  the ${\mathrm{Cu/GMP}}$ MOF  laccase  mimic. Wang et al. fabricated another Cu-based laccase mimic to break down phenolic contaminants by substituting the GMP ligand with a cysteine-histidine dipeptide [74].
## 2.3 SOD
A class of enzymes known as SOD can catalyze the dismutation of unstable  superoxide  radicals  into $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ .  Qu’s  team developed  a  Cu-TCPP  MOF  with  SOD-like  activity  inspired  by the active components of natural $\mathrm{Cu}Z\mathrm{n-SOD}$ [75]. The Cu-TCPP nanocomposites  were  sonicated  into  tiny  nanodots  to  enhance their  catalytic  activity.  These  dots  have  the  same  size  as  natural enzymes  and  offer  more  accessible  catalytic  sites  for  substrates with lower diffusion barriers than nanosheets, which may be taken out by the renal route, and significantly reduce the toxicity of the nanozymes.  The  effectiveness  of  the  Cu-TCPP  MOF  nanodots serving  as  a  synthetic  SOD  in  minimizing  endotoxemia  was validated  by in  vitro and in  vivo studies,  and  they  revealed extremely minimal cytotoxicity.
## 2.4    Hydrolases
A  group  of  enzymes  known  as  hydrolases  can  catalyze  several hydrolysis  processes.  Certain  MOF-based  materials  may  imitate three  primary  types  of  hydrolases:  organophosphorus  hydrolase, protease,  and  esterase,  depending  on  the  target  substrates. Research  has  revealed  that $Z\mathrm{r}$ -based  MOFs  may  successfully dissolve the phosphonate ester interaction [76–82]. Most evidence suggests that the $Z\mathrm{r}$ -based MOF hydrolase-mimetic activity must be related to their $\mathrm{Zr-O}$ clusters’ robust and precise interactions with the phosphate group in substrates. Farha’s group used a $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{-}$ based  MOF  called  NU-1000  to  efficiently  hydrolyze  chemical warfare  chemicals  with  phosphonate  ester  linkages  [76, 83]. H4TBAPy  is  1,3,6,8  tetrakis  (p-benzoic  acid)  pyrene  ligand  and eight linked $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}(\mathrm{m}_{3}{-}\mathrm{O})_{4}(\mathrm{m}_{3}{-}\mathrm{OH})_{4}(\mathrm{-OH}_{2})_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}$ nodes to form the NU-1000. Larger phosphate ester molecules can pass through the anticipated  NU-1000  because  ultra-high  pores  and  big  channels saturate the framework reaction. Under the catalysis of the NU1000  nanozyme,  dimethyl  4-nitrophenyl  phosphate  (DMNP), which  served  as  the  substrate,  was  hydrolyzed  into  p-nitro phenoxide  anions  and  phosphate  at $\mathrm{\pH}=10\$ with  a  turnover frequency (TOF) as strong as $0.06\ s^{-1}$ . Several MOFs are reported to exhibit the ability to catalyze the degradation of proteins aside from organophosphorus hydrolase. Li’s team synthesized two Cubased MOFs and discovered that they possessed inherent proteaselike catalytic activity for protein degradation [44, 84]. According to the findings, the HKUST-1 MOF had a Michaelis constant $(K_{\mathrm{m}})_{\mathrm{\ell}}$ such  as  26,000  times  lower  than  trypsin’s,  and  showed  greater affinity for bovine serum albumin (BSA) [44].
Additionally, the insoluble MOF-based nanozymes are easy to recycle by centrifugation and can be reused without losing activity. Furthermore,  Chen  et  al.  were  encouraged  by  the  content  and structure  of  human  carbonic  anhydrase  II  (HCAII),  and  several zeolitic  imidazolate  frameworks  (ZIFs),  which  can  successfully catalyze  the  hydrolysis  of  esters,  were  designed  and  developed [85].  The  structure  of  HCAII  comprises  three  symmetric molecules such as histidine, imidazoles, and water. The $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ion is tetrahedrally coordinated (Fig. 3(a)). The ZIF-8 material incorporated four 2-methyl imidazolates (2-mIM) ligands with a core $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ion  identical  to  HCAII  [86].  The  tetrahedrally coordinated $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ion was combined to fabricate the highly porous framework using 2-mIM. The metal MOF material demonstrated comparable esterase-like properties to catalyze the hydrolysis of pnitrophenyl  acetate  (pNPA),  owing  to  the  similarity  in composition and geometric structure (Fig. ${3(\mathrm{b})}\ddot{}$ ).
## 2.5    Multi-enzyme assembly
In addition to the MOFs, as mentioned earlier, several MOFs can demonstrate multi-enzyme performances under various circumstances or even under the same condition [17, 44, 87, 88]. Characteristics  function  like  more  than  one  enzyme  in  a  single MOF to form a multi-enzyme material assembly offering one with a  combined  instrument  for  cascade  catalysis  and  biomedical treatments. Our team generally produced a $\mathrm{Cu}$ -BDC MOF with POD  and  cysteine  oxidase-mimetic  capabilities  [88].  The $\mathrm{Cu-O}$ clusters in the MOF showed the potential to catalyze the oxidation of cysteine to yield cystine and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ with the help of dissolved oxygen. Still, they also made exhaustive efforts to mimic POD to catalyze the production of hydroxide radicals from the produced $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ in the same buffer, which further oxidized the BDC ligand in the material to produce a fluorescence signal. We were able to identify  the  cysteine  molecule  with  ease  and  excellent  efficiency due  to  the  Cu-BDC  MOF’s  cascade  reaction  and  stimulusresponsive fluorescence.
## 3    Structures, composite, and properties of MOFbased nanozymes
## 3.1    Structures of MOF-based nanozyme
## 3.1.1    Three-dimensional structure of MOFs nanozyme
The published research proposes that the metal nodes such as $\mathrm{Ag,}$ Fe,  Zr,  Cu,  Zn,  etc.,  can  get  several  functional  groups  when bonded  with  organic  ligands,  for  instance,  sulphonic  acid, carboxyl,  and  phosphate  functional  groups,  to  fabricate  MOFs with  outstanding  catalytic  efficiency  and  extensive  organized structure [25, 89]. The 3D MOFs contain pigments such as heme called PCN-222(Fe) as a ligand that was designed and synthesized by Feng et al. [90] by using a ligand termed Fe-TCPP tetrakis(4- carboxyphenyl)porphyrin  and  architecturally  stable $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ clustered as secondary building units (Fig. 4(a)).
This  PCN  containing $3.7\ \mathrm{nm}$ diameter  crystalline  porous network  structures  is  used  as  a  stable  enzyme  mimic. Furthermore,  using  the  superior  adsorption  of  PCN-222(Fe)  for tetra  bromobisphenol  A  (TBBPA)  in  an  aqueous  solution,  Yu et  al.  produced  an  easy  carbon  paste  electrode  based  on  PCN222(Fe) and acetylene black for the analysis of TBBPA with high sensitivity  [91].  By  using  colorimetric  detection  to  identify  the presence  of  molecules  in  biomedical  samples,  the  3D  MOF nanozymes,  like  biological  catalysts,  may  be  more  thoroughly investigated. In addition to developing a colorimetric identification  substrate  by  adding $^{3,3^{\prime},5,5^{\prime}}$ -tetramethylbenzidine (TMB) used for glucose, Aghayan et al. [92] produced PCN-222. They utilized its enzymatic activity to identify $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ following the former process. From earlier findings on POD replications, it is clear  that  enhancing  the  charge  transport  between  the  substrate and catalyst would improve the catalysis of enzyme mimics [93]. The developed nanozyme may induce color changes in solution by  reacting  with  the  substrates $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -azino-bis-(3-ethylbenzo thiazoline-6-sulfonate)  (AzBTS)  and  TMB  o-phenylenediamine using its inherent POD activity.
An  organic–inorganic  MOFs-aptamer  hybridization  system developed  by  Wang  et  al.  [94]  initially  covalently  attached ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ and  4,4-bipyridine  to  produce  Cu-MOFs  with  POD-mimicking catalytic activity. Strong pyrophosphate (PPi) combined with ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ affinity  inhibits  enzymatic  activity  under  usual  conditions. However, PPi hydrolyzes to orthophosphate (Pi) when interacting with ALP, yielding ABTS+ with color (Fig. $4(\mathrm{b})_{,}^{\cdot}$ ). Liang et al. [95] produced  an  amorphous  MOF  material  of  laccase  mimics  by using simple coordination of GMP and $\mathrm{Cu},$ denoted as ${\mathrm{Cu/GMP}}$ nanozyme,  where  the  stoichiometric  ratio  of GMP  to  Cu  is  4:3 (Fig. 4(c)). Compared to protein laccase, ${\mathrm{Cu/GMP}}$ nanozyme has a  comparable $K_{\mathrm{m}}$ and  a  greater $V_{\mathrm{max}}$ while  costing  about 2400 times less. ${\mathrm{Cu/GMP}}$ nanozymes with strong catalytic activity have  been  proven  to  be  generated  from  the  ligand  guanosine, which  has  greater  stability  in  severe  conditions,  making  it attractive in analytical chemistry and environmental applications. For colorimetric sensing measurement of enzymes besides glucose and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ by  substituting  the  aptamer/target  species  attached  to MOFs based on properties in the catalytic reaction to TMB, FeMIL-88A (charged ion-based MOF material) exhibits a blue color. These properties are due to the aptamer that may selectively bind the biomolecules and produce a compound that can enclose FeMIL-88A,  preventing  the  transfer  of  electrons  with  TMB  and inhibiting  enzyme-mimetic  activities  and  ions $\left(\mathrm{Cu^{+}}\right)$ .  Through glutathione  (GSH)  exhaustion  and  auto-oxidation,  they  work together to increase the generation of intracellular radical oxidative species  (ROS).  As  a  result,  oxidative  stress  rises,  drug-resistant tumor cells’ cellular redox equilibrium changes, and various drugresistant tumor cells are preferentially eliminated (Fig. 4(d)). Wei’s group  [67]  synthesized  the  light-responsive  rod-like  MOF nanocomposites  with  high  oxidase-like  activity  by  using $\mathrm{{Ru(bpy)}}_{3}^{2+}$ byproducts  as  polystyrene  ligands  by  mixing $^{4,4^{\prime}.}$ - diphenyldicarboxylicacid $\mathrm{(H_{2}B P D C)}$ and $Z\mathrm{n}$ nitrate hexa hydrate $(\mathrm{ZnNO}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ in dimethylformamide (DMF).
The drawback of traditional inorganic photoactive materials for visible  light  was  resolved  with  low  light  absorption  intensity  by successfully controlling the enzymatic activities of the nanozymes using  only  a  power  switch.  The  process  uses  organic  dyes  as ligands for MOFs. It allows the integration of many linkers into the  distinct  crystal  pores  of  MOFs,  which  can  prohibit  organic dyes from degrading and dimerizing due to oxidation in aqueous solutions. It is effective for preventing biomolecule contamination in the experiment. They applied electronic spin resonance (ESR) spectroscopy for analysis to mimic the enzyme’s mode of action. They  found  that  the  oxidase  mimics  the  activities  of  substances and catalyzes the generation of ROS, primarily OH and $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . Based on these materials, the researchers designed a colorimetric sensor that can detect ROS in both healthy and cancerous cells and that is sensitive,  tunable,  and  light-responding.  This  study  has significantly  expanded  the  spectrum  of  sensing  applications  for manufactured nanozymes.
## 3.1.2    Two-dimensional MOFs-based nanozymes
The 2D MOF nanosheets are new participants of the MOF-based material, which exhibit several characteristics associated with their structures that are different from those of 3D MOFs. They have gained  popularity  in  catalysis,  biosensing,  electrochemistry,  and other  fields  [98].  There  are  two  methods  for  constructing  2D MOFs, top-down and bottom-up. In contrast, the second mostly directs  synthesis,  and  several  approaches  were  given  to  advance the  effective  fabrication  of  2D  MOFs.  The  published  strategies involve  the  interfacial  based  on  these  materials,  exfoliation (chemical  and  ultrasonic  process),  template  synthesis,  and surfactant-assisted  fabrication  techniques.  M-TCPP  nanosheets $(\mathrm{M}=\mathrm{Cu},$ , Co, Zn, and Cd) with thicknesses less than $10\ \mathrm{nm}$ were effectively  synthesized  first  time  using  surfactant-assisted fabrication technique by Zhao et al. [99]. Several DNAs may be detected when kept in the fluorescence detection system for DNA fluorescence identification. Additionally, its outstanding performance was generally produced by its unique 2D structure, which experiments have proved. The ferroptosis inhibitory protein 1  (FSP1)  and  glutathione  peroxidase  4  (GPX4)  are  two biochemical processes regulating lipid peroxide decontamination and  preventing  cellular  iron  death.  Cellular  iron  death  is  a controlled cellular death (CD). Li et al. [100] first incorporated Au NPs with Cu-TCPP(Fe) MOF via in situ nucleation nanomaterials,  then  packed  them  with  GSH  POD  inhibitor (RSL3) via $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking, and then produced the stimulant to the tumor  cells  via  surface  modifications,  resulting  in  significant cellular iron death via the inhibitory activity of GPX4/GSH and $\mathrm{FSP1/CoQ10H_{2}}$ (Fig. 5(a)). 2D MOFs in the MOF group can be integrated with inorganic materials. The chemical combination of the  organic  ligands,  in  nature  with  them,  may  speed  up  the movement  of  charges,  which  encourages  the  separating  and transport of photoinduced ions and improves the semiconductor’s photocatalytic properties. Liang et al. achieved this by coating the exterior surface of $\mathrm{Ag_{3}P O_{4}}$ NPs, with an incredibly thin layer of MOFs  (UMOFNs).  Liang  et  al.  [101, 102]  were  able  to  produce photocatalysts  with  core–shell  architectures $(\mathrm{Ag}_{3}\mathrm{PO}_{4})$ .  Under visible light, the UMOFNs exhibit a strong breakdown potential for  the  organic  contaminant  phenol.  Enzymatically  active  2D layered material includes double-layered hydroxides (often called hydrotalcite,  layered  double  hydroxides  (LDHs))  and  layered nanocomposite  oxides  (LCOs),  composed  of  different  cations layers with interlayer charge balancing negative ions. This material can  also  have  excellent  catalytic  activity  when  combined  with MOFs.  Hu  et  al.  fabricated  a  ZIF-8/Zn-Al  layer  double  oxide (LDO) composite by encapsulating ZIF-8 on a Zn-Al LDO surface with in situ growth [103]. This material can adsorb and degrade methylene  blue  (MB)  dye  through  photocatalysis,  producing  a promising  material  for  the  photocatalytic  breakdown  of  waste water  management.  Khoobi  et  al.  [104]  manufactured  and developed  ZIF-8  from  LDHs  with  equivalent  characteristics  and produced  the  layered  nanomaterials,  and  that  material  has  been utilized  to  identify  penicillin  (penicillin  G)  by  using  an in  situ HPLC-UV  evaluation  process  based  on  stir  bar  sorption extraction  (SBSE)  [104–106].  Additionally,  by  modifying  the surface of 2D MOF nanomaterials, the organic ligands may confer certain  functionalities,  which  depend  on  improving  inherent enzyme-like  characteristics.  Using  a  bottom-up  approach,  Hu et  al.  [107]  effectively  manufactured  amino-modified  2D  MOF nanoflakes with a thickness of only $4.2\mathrm{nm}$ . These nanoflakes were used  to  develop  a  bioluminescent  sensor  with  inherent characteristics like enzyme and fluorescent characteristics in fish samples to detect and quantify hypoxanthine (Hx). Shi et al. [108] used the inherent POD activity of 2D $\mathrm{Cu(bpy)}_{2}(\mathrm{OTf})_{2}$ nanoflakes to obtain the first fluorescence monitoring of glucose and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . Thiamine, a non-fluorescent substrate, was transformed into the intensely  fluorescent  thiochromone  (TC)  in  a  basic  medium  to accomplish  this.  This  technique  has  been  effectively  used  as  a clinical diagnostic standard to determine the amount of glucose in serum  samples.  By  using  a  surfactant-assisted  approach  to fabricate 2D Cu-TCPP(Fe) nanolayers which were then modified with Pt NPs, depending on the POD mimetic performance of Pt NPs/Cu-TCPP(Fe),  Chen  et  al.  [109]  designed  a  colorimetric approach  for  detecting $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ .  Using  glucose  binding,  a  very sensitive cascade colorimetric test for glucose has been devised in Fig. 5(b).  Additionally,  Chen  et  al.  [110]  used  a  solvothermal approach  to  prepare  Ni-based  nanoflakes  with  POD  mimic function.  This  method  catalyzed  the  reaction  of  TMB  with hydrogen peroxide to prepare a blue, visible final product to the naked eye. Hydrogen peroxide in human serum may be detected with  high  selectivity  and  sensitivity.  Additionally, in  vivo diagnostics  in  animals,  2D  MOF  nanosheets  can  simulate  POD activity. Using $\mathrm{CO^{2+}}$ , $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ , and ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ with TCPP ligand, Wei’s team [47]  fabricated  several  metallated  2D  MOFs  in  2017.  The  most active 2D MOF was TCPP(Fe), demonstrating the predominance of  ligands  generated  from  porphyrins  in  enzymatic  activity. Utilizing  the  anti-heparin  (Hep)  peptide  AG73’s  selective adsorption  on  the  MOF  and  preventing  the  place  at  which  the catalyst  interacts  with  the  specific  substrate  linkages,  they constructed  sensitive  Hep  test  probe  by  applying  this  approach.
The inhibitory influences of adenosine diphosphate (ADP), $\mathrm{{PPi},}$ and  adenosine  triphosphate  (ATP),  on  the  2D  MOFs  POD mimetic properties, were then effectively used to develop a sensing array comprised of three 2D MOFs for identification of ALP, and dynamic range recognition (Fig. 5(c)) [111, 112]. To improve the catalytic and sensing characteristics of M NPs, 2D nanoflakes with adjacent  dimensions  may  correspondingly  be  utilized  like substrates. Compared to bulk MOFs, 2D MOFs can offer many reactive  sites  for  metal  NPs  and  enhance  charge  transport’s effectiveness. To determine glucose in human serum, Chen et al. [113]  synthesized  a  biosensor  by  manufacturing  Au-Ni nanomaterials functionalized with a glassy carbon electrode (GCE) highly  sensitive  for  glucose  oxidation  through  electrostatic interaction of Au NPs onto the surface of a nickel-based MOFs. Yan et al. fabricated composites with POD and GOx capability for detecting biomolecules [114]. They first synthesized the 2D hybrid MOFs nanosheets (MOFNs-Ni), then they coated the MOFNs-Ni with  Ag  NPs  through  impregnation.  Finally,  the  nanozyme  was applied to reduce the nitrobenzol water. The generated composites may  perform  the  catalytic  cascade  process  by  not  mixing  the natural  enzymes  by  hybridizing  2D  nanomaterials  with  various enzymatic  activities.  Tan  et  al.  [115]  developed  2D  bimetallic MOFNs at normal temperatures, and the bimetallic configuration enhanced  its  attraction  for  single-stranded  DNA  (ssDNA).  The conformational  modification  to  the  single-stranded  structure changed  the  electrostatic  attraction,  adjusting  and  achieving catalytic performance using DNA. It significantly increases the use of  2D  MOFNs  in  colorimetric  sensing.  Multi-walled  carbon nanotubes (MWCNTs) were incorporated by Wang et al. [116]. 2D  M-TCPP $\mathrm{(Cu)}$ nanolayers  as  charge  transport  substrate  with the  weight  ratio  of  1.5:1  and  the  efficiency  of  MOF  electron transport may be increased by adding MWCNTs (Fig. 5(d)). The Langmuir–Schäfer technique was successfully applied to the GCE to  fabricate  nanocomposite  films  with  hydrogen  peroxide electrochemical activity. The film-functionalized gas chromatographic  electrodes  were  applied  to  determine  the concentration of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ in actual samples. This investigation makes it possible to fabricate additional composites based on 2D MOFNs for the usage of electrochemistry applications, and to increase the electrocatalytic activity of 2D MOFs nanosheets. Furthermore, the 2D MOFNs exhibit improved characteristics as derivatives of raw material.  By  using  the  easy  bottom-up  method,  porphyrin  cosubstrate  paddle  wheel  frame-3  (PPF-3)  was  fabricated  by  Zhai et al. [117].
## 3.2    MOFs-based composite nanozyme
## 3.2.1    MOFs/metal particle composite nanozymes
Previous  studies  about  catalysts  focused  on  pure  metals.  Most transition  metals  were  used  as  catalysts  due  to  empty  d-orbital, chemical, mechanical and thermal stability, and the high surface area  of  the  active  catalyst.  Hence  all  these  properties  can  be improved by loading the metals on a carrier and contact between the  carrier  and  metal,  which  will  modify  the  catalytic  efficiency. The noble metal NPs catalysts that use MOFs as support materials have drawn much interest in the last ten years. Ag, Au, and Pt NPs are  known  as  noble  metal  NPs,  often  utilized  in  biomolecular identification, antibacterial treatment, and environmental control, among  other  applications,  and  have  strong  enzyme-mimetic activity.  Due  to  the  uniform  and  adjustable  pore  diameter  of MOF, high surface area, extensively exposed active sites, and ease of functionalization, composite catalysts with various characteristics may be produced by freely combining MOFs and metal  NPs.  The  studied  catalytic  materials  mostly  use  the precursor  approach  and  metal  NPs  process  [29, 118–122].  As  a sensitive analytical method, the SERS has several uses in biological investigation.  The  fabrication  of  good  SERS  active  materials significantly  impacts  Raman  intensity.  Using  plasmonic  nanocharged particles with an increased Raman signal, Au and Ag NPs are  commonly  applied  like  SERS  substrates  [123].  Wei’s  team advanced  the  creation  of  effective  malachite  green  (MG)  with increasing Au NPs in situ on MIL-101 green Raman signal; the Au NPs@MIL-101@GOx,  and  Au  NPs@MIL-101@LOx  lactate oxidase, incorporated NPs arranged by GOx and LOx were used to trace lactate, and glucose in vitro (Fig. 6(a)). First of all, MOFs functionalized  by  Ag  NPs  by  utilizing  an in  situ technique  (Ag NPs/MIL-101(Fe))  were  produced  by  Jiang  et  al.  [124]  and employed  like  new  SERS  substrates  by  improved  SERS  outputs. The several Raman spikes amongst the main Ag NPs offer suitable environments  for  efficient  quantities  of  analytes.  To  detect dopamine  (DA)  with  extreme  sensitivity, $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -azino  bis(3- ethylbenzthiazoline-6-sulfonic acid), an ultrasensitive ammonium salt (ABTS), was used as a POD-conjugated immunosorbent assay (ELISA) substrate. Sun et al. [52] made the polymerization of DA in an aqueous solution on the surface of MIL-101(Cr) to generate the  copolymers  (dopamine)  (PDA),  which  then  underwent  in reduction process with $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{g}^{+}$ ions to produce $\mathrm{AgNPs/MIL}{\cdot}\mathrm{101}\mathrm{(Cr)}$ as  a  SERS  substrate  for  the  identification  of  folate  (FA).  Above mentioned findings can offer the method of expanding biomedical uses  of  the  SERS  technology.  Under  the  theory  of  meritocratic crystal formation, new topologies are produced during the crystal nucleation process by modifying the polar solvent. According to this theory, Sun et al. first fabricated several kinds of ZIF-67 (ultrathin structures and dodecahedral). Then, the $\mathrm{AgNPs}$ were placed at  ZIF-67  to  improve  GCE.  They  fabricated  sensor  by  these materials,  and  applied  for  recognition  of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ in  HepG2 hepatoma  cells.  Shen  et  al.  developed  an  electrochemical  sensor with high sensitivity by modifying Ce-MOFs with Au NPs [125]. They  determine  the  lipopolysaccharide  (LPS)  by  surface functionalization Ce-MOFs with Au NPs, and capture the hairpin probe 2 (HP2) with Au NPs/Ce-MOFs. The twofold amplification design  technique  provides  high  sensitivity  and  a  low  detection limit  for  the  detection  of  LPS.  The  electronic  interaction  can considerably increase the catalytic activity between the surface of the sheets of carbon and the metal core. Cobalt (Co) is densely deposited inside the mesoporous channels structure, creating an abundance of active sites. Due to these sites, they were developed as colorimetric biosensors for determining of glucose concentration in samples. Attributed to increased overall catalytic efficiency,  MOFs  are  modified  with  Pt  NPs,  and  Pt-based nanocomposites,  which  have  strong  enzymatic  performance  for imitating catalysts. Deng et al. encapsulated Pt NPs with several Zrbased MOFs (UiO-66, 67, and 68) [126]. The catalytic efficiency of the  above  catalysts  was  evaluated  by $\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ probe.  The  results revealed  that  enzymatic  performance  of  these  composites  is  the following: $\mathrm{Pt@UiO_{\delta}~<~\mathrm{Pt@UiO_{\delta}68_{-}2~<~67P t@U i O_{\delta}662.}}$ By utilizing  an  organic  ligand  named  a  2-amino  terephthalic  acid $(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{-H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC})$ ),  Yang  et  al.  [127]  first  fabricated ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ centered $\operatorname{Co}(\operatorname{II})$ MOFs, after which they used $\mathrm{Pt-N}$ bonding to immobilize a  certain  number  of  Pt  NPs,  and  eventually  synthesized  Pt $\mathrm{{NPs@Co(II)}}$ and MOFs@Pt NPs composites. They demonstrated robust  reduction  and  oxidation  reactions  and  catalytic characteristics. They served as a carrier to immobilize TBA II, a modified  electrode  surface,  using  a  sandwich  structure  and produced  charge  transport  by  the  movement  of  electrons  from ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ to ${\mathrm{Co}}^{3+}$ .  This  led  to  realization  of  electrochemical  signal intensification. $\mathrm{PtNP@OUiO-66\mathrm{-}N H_{2}}$ nanomaterials  composite with POD-like characteristics was successfully prepared by Li et al. [128]. These nanomaterials have outstanding specific adsorption capability for $\mathrm{Hg^{2+}}$ in the aqueous medium. The results imply that these nanomaterials can be used for the colorimetric detection of $\mathrm{Hg^{2+}}$ in drinking water with high sensitivity and effectiveness (Fig. $6(\mathrm{b})_{.}^{\cdot}$ ). To prepare $\mathrm{Au}@\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) hybrid NPs, Zhang et al. [129] combined the impregnation approach with the borohydride elimination  technique.  These  NPs  have  better  selectivity  for  the substrates  than  HRPs.  Based  on  this,  a  multifunctional colorimetric  monitoring  system  was  developed  and  applied  to detect cysteine in genuine bovine serum and $\mathrm{Hg^{2+}}$ in groundwater (Fig. $6(\mathrm{c}))$ . With Au NPs-modified MOF hybrid materials, Wang et al. [130] detected $\mathrm{Hg^{2+}}$ in water with many different conditions. The  above  findings  supported  the  enormous  potential  of  Au nanomaterial-functionalized  with  MOFS  due  to  environmental safety applications. To prevent Pt NP self-aggregating, Zhang et al. functionalized  Pt  NPs  by  porphyrin-based  MOF  [131].  The fabricated  substances  act  as  a  catalyst  displayed  POD-like enzymatic  activity.  It  can  convert  endogenous $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ into $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and produce tumor-killing singlet oxygen $\left(^{1}\mathrm{O}_{2}\right)$ , which improved the therapeutic  effect  of  photodynamic  therapy  (PDT)  that  is appropriate  for  different  processes  (Fig. $6(\mathrm{d})_{\cdot}$ ).  Wei’s  team projected  an  artificial  approach  for  SOD/catalase  (SOD/CAT) cascade  nanozymes  (Pt@PCN222-Mn)  with  SOD-like  Mn(III) and  CAT-like  Pt  NPs  [132].  They  demonstrated  increased  ROS elimination  and  have  the  potential  to  treat  Crohn’s  disease  and inflammatory bowel diseases (IBD). Furthermore, Han et al. [133] described  encapsulating  Au  nanorods  (Au  NRs)  wrapped  with silicon shells to allow antibacterial iodine storage and controlled release. As mentioned earlier, the study convincingly shows that MOF-based composite materials using metal NPs have many uses in biosensing.
## 3.2.2    MOFs/metal oxide composite nanozymes
Metal oxides are composed of more than two positive ions with different valences for instance, manganese tetraoxide, ferric oxide, cerium  oxide,  and  vanadium  pentoxide  possess  like  enzymatic properties,  which  mimics  the  enzymes  and  can  convert  oxygen from  their  composite  to  reactants  molecule  [29, 135–139].  The fundamental  cause  of  the  enzymatic  activity  is  the  interaction between the NPs and the reactant substrate, which is coupled by an  electron  transfer  and  valence  change  and  often  exhibits oxidoreductase  activity  and  strong  environmental  stability [140–142].  Nevertheless,  the  enzymatic  activity  is  constrained since  only  the  exposed  atoms  on  the  NP’s  surface  are  engaged. Combining the metal oxides and MOFs is necessary to increase enzymatic  activity  [143].  After  bonding,  they  can  be  used  for electrochemical  sensing,  tumor  treatment,  catalytic  alcohol oxidation  processes,  SERS  reactions,  and  bioanalytical  and quantitative  tests.  The  reported  work  represented  that  the magnetic ferric oxide NPs exhibited inherent POD-like activity in 2007  [144].  The  word “nanozyme” was  called  synthetic  NPs, which have activity similar to natural enzymes [145, 146]. Proteins have  many  parameters  of  process  and  substrate  content requirements  as  compared  to  the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ NPs.  Fu  et  al.  [147] constructed  three  different $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoparticle  architectures comprising  nanodiamonds,  nanoflowers,  and  nanoclusters  and examined  their  activity  by  altering  the $\mathrm{\pH}$ value  during  the hydrothermal process. They next investigated the catalytic activity of nanodiamond $\angle$ nanoflowers $<$ nanoclusters in this sequence. Numerous  research  teams  have  tried  a  variety  of  valuable integrating  materials,  such  as  coupling $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ to  MOFs incorporating  enzyme-mimetic  characteristics  to  produce  more active nanomaterials. Zhao et al. [148] and Tang et al. fabricated crystalline  nanomaterials,  which  contain  MOFs  and $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ $(\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/M I L–101(F e)})$ [149]. That nanomaterial was mainly coated with magnetic $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\mathrm{NPs}$ at the surface of the MOF structure by interactions  between  electrostatic  charges.  Without  adding catalysts,  the  material  can  be  utilized  directly  for  the  luminol  of chemiluminescence  [150].  The  efficiency  of  enzymes  can  be enhanced significantly by separating parent material single units, which can be used in neutral Fenton-like mixture structures with significant  activation  of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ lacking  extra  energy  input  and favorable  for  water  remedy  procedure  of  progressive  oxidation method.
Moreover,  for  the  quantifiable  measurements  of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and ${\mathrm{C}}_{6}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}{\mathrm{O}}_{6},$ the $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/M I L\cdot101(F e)}$ was designed as ultra-sensitivity sensors. In addition, these sensors are magnificently used in the disinfection of drugs and determination of human serum in blood (Fig. 7(a)).  Lai  et  al.  fabricated  the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{Au}@\mathrm{MIL}{-}100(\mathrm{Fe})$ hybrids [146]. According to Lai et al. [151], the core structure of their MIL-100(Fe)-nature MOF shell is on top of a magnetically interlayer that is rich in interparticle hot spots for environmental monitoring  applications,  and  the  MOF  shell  (Fig. 7(b))  may  be employed  as  a  new  SERS  active  substrate  and  has  tremendous worth.  To  accelerate  the  degree  of  substrate  transportation  in multidimensional catalysis by uniform metallization on $Z\mathrm{r}$ -based metal clusters, Cui et al. [147] inserted a vanadium oxide catalyst (V-NU-1000) comprised of $Z\mathrm{r}$ -based MOF as a carrier. It has been demonstrated  through  experimentation  that  the  catalyst  exhibits high  stability,  selectivity,  and  resistance  to  deactivation  by  metal leaching  in  liquid  phase  conditions  when  utilized  for  alcohol oxidation reactions. To combat the negative impacts of the tumor microenvironment (TME) on photodynamic treatment, Yin et al. [152]  designed  a  nano  platform  that  included  porphyrin-based MOFs as photosensitizers and ferromanganese oxide NPs acting as nanozymes.
To improve PDT treatment, it burns GSH and prevents ROS consumption (Fig. 7(c)). Hydrogen peroxide is evenly catalyzed to produce oxygen in the oxygen-deficient tumor microenvironment. Next, ROS is catalyzed in the microenvironment to enable the efficient death of tumor cells and to  enhance  the  treatment  role  of  oxygen  to  accomplish  an anticancer  activity.  This  work  also  offers  innovative  thought  for fabricating  a  tandem-based  uniform  MOF-based  catalyst  for combined  treatment.  The  main  advantage  of  magnetic  MOFs (MMOFs) is their isolation from the reaction mixture. Still, when utilized  as  a  biosensor,  they  have  the  drawback  of  having  poor contact  with  the  electrode  surface.  Lu  et  al.  [153]  fabricated  an electrochemical process-based biosensor by binding Au nanoflowers to $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{ZIF}-8$ stabilized by $\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ through one-step electrochemical  deposition.  The  conservation  of  the  MMOF surface  state  and  the  thin  gold  layers  are  critical  for  the electrocatalytic stability and activity of the MMOF (Fig. 7(d)).
## 3.2.3    MOFs-based sulfide nanocomposite nanozymes
High-performance  hydrogen  evolution  reaction  (HER)  catalysts may be prepared with enormous electrolyte penetration channels with the uniform special structure of sulfides and the vacant $3\mathrm{p}$ unoccupied  orbitals  in  sulphur  atoms  utilized  for  capturing  and transporting  electrons.  These  are  frequently  employed  in bioanalysis, content analysis, protection of the environment, and electrochemical  detection.  Multilayered $\mathrm{CuS}@C$ non-precious metal catalysts with high HER enzymatic activity and long-term stability  were  constructed  in Fig. 8(a).  Firstly,  sulfate  2D  Cucontaining  MOF  (Cu-BDC  nanosheets)  was  fabricated  by  Rong et  al.  [154].  The  empty  3p  orbital  of $\mathrm{CuS}$ offered  a  penetration route for the species, which enhanced transferring and capturing of an electron and the morphological features of the 2D Cu-BDC precursor itself. It serves as a template for producing 2D MOFs precursor  for  high-performance  electrochemical  catalysts.  To facilitate the efficient transfer of electrons, initially, Wei et al. [29] packed calcined MIL-68 (cMIL-68) onto the GCE coated in $\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ nanolayers,  then  packed  Pt  NPs  on  the  surface  of  cMIL$68/\mathrm{MoS}_{2}/\mathrm{GCE}$ through electrodeposition. The fabricated nanostructures  have  unusual  electrocatalytic  activity  and  are suitable  for  measuring $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ content  released  when  drugs stimulate  MCF-7  breast  cancer  cells  (Fig. 8(b)).  This  research might  be  used  to  develop  an  enzyme-free  biosensor  for  an intracellular drug test. Attached MIL-101(Fe) with enhanced POD enzymatic  activity  (symbolized  as $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ -MIL-101(Fe))  has  been effectively synthesized by Dong et al. [155].
Moreover,  MIL-101(Fe)  is  highly  porous  for ${\mathrm{MoS}}_{2},$ ,  which encourages the absorption of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and TMB through $\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ and may  raise  the  active  unit  of  MIL-101.  The  hydrodynamic  MIL101(Fe)  fraction  of  the $\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ -MIL-101(Fe)  structure  reduces  the Mo  agglomeration  of  Fe  [156].  A  sensitive  technique  for identifying $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ has been developed to detect blood glucose levels accurately.  By  wetting  down  Cu-based  MOF,  Xiong  et  al.  [157] effectively  produced  CuS  (PCuS)  particles  (HKUST-1).  The surface  of  PCuS  serves  as  the  system  for  identifying  and quantifying $\mathrm{Hg^{2+}}$ and  has  a  strong  affinity  for $\mathrm{Hg^{2+}}$ ,  and  the resulting  mercury  sulfides  suppress  the  POD  activity  of $\mathsf{P C u S.}$ . Using its high surface area and strong relationship for $\mathrm{Hg^{2+}}$ , PCuS may also be employed as an efficient adsorbent for $\mathrm{Hg^{2+}}$ from an environmental point of view.
## 3.2.4    MOF/metal ion composite nanozyme
Typically,  MOFs  may  be  chemically  functionalized  by  adding active  materials  to  unoccupied  metal  sites  by  multifunctional modification with a few metal nodes or organic linkers [160–163]. The Lewis acid effect of metal ions enables the substrate to become directly polarized by accepting electrons, allowing hydroxide ions or  water  molecules  to  target  the  substrate  and  easily  produce adsorbate  breakdown  [164].  Metals  can  also  serve  as  a  bridge between  MOFs  and  substrate  atoms  contact,  which  enables  the enzymatic reaction. Ion doping might give MOFs properties like enzymatic  chemiluminescence  or  enhance  the  electrochemical signal  by  materials.  However,  by  adding  nanostructured modulators like silica shells, polymers, and phospholipids on the surface of MOF structure, it is possible to overcome the drawbacks of pristine MOF, for instance, instability; both improvements can somehow enhance the capability of MOFs to mimic an enzyme by adding  materials  with  specific  functional  properties,  and  postsynthetic  modification  (PSM)  is  a  technique  for  improving  the morphology of the fabricated MOFs and achieving the necessary physicochemical characteristics. Chen et al. [165] constructed UIO form composite material (NMOFs) employing 2,2'-bipyridine- $^{5,5^{\prime}}$ - dicarboxylic acid as a linker with $\mathrm{zr}$ ions and the bipyridine ligand as ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ binding  sites.  In  the  presence  of $\mathrm{luminol/H_{2}O_{2}},$ the synthesized ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}.$ -NMOFs could catalyze the oxidation of Amplexred,  dopamine  to  produce  fluorescent  test  halide  (Resorufin) amido  chromes  or  chemiluminescence.  When  fluorescein  was doped,  an  effective  CRET  procedure  was  created,  catalyzed  by
NMOF, and occurred in the presence of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . UIO-type NMOFs that have been post-synthesized with $\mathrm{Cu^{2+}}$ were also employed as sensors.  To  identify  bacterial  LPS,  Li  et  al.  [166]  synthesized  a polyfunctional ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ NMOFs  electrolytic  biosensor.  Dapping  the ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ improved  the  material’s  electrolytic  response.  The  anion exchange  group  preferentially  collected  the ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ -NMOFs  on  the LPS molecule utilizing GCE modified with Au NPs as substrate and C18 alkane thiol chains as a link between the Au NPs and LPS. Zhou et al. [167] introduced organic molecules of selenium (phenylselenyl bromide, PhSeBr) into the $Z\mathrm{r}$ -based MOF (UIO-66- $\mathrm{NH}_{2})$ structure through covalent PSM strategy by MOF pore to provide  the  catalytically  active  premises  and  selenium  as  a  fully functioning donor [168]. The fabricated nanozyme may have high enzymatic  activity  as  a $\mathrm{GPx}$ mimic  afterward  repeated  periods. Our  findings  suggest  that  the  PSM  technique  might  be  a  great strategy for creating effective natural enzyme mimics depending on MOFs [169].
## 3.3    Properties of MOFs-based nanozymes
## 3.3.1    Size of pore
The MOFs-based nanozymes have high porosity, and it has been investigated that the size of the pore influences the activity of their characteristics.  Wang  et  al.  prepared  mesoporous  Fe-based porphyrins-consisting MOFs (PCN-600(Fe)). These POD mimics have one-dimensional (1D) layers with lengths of up to $3.1\ \mathrm{nm}$ Furthermore,  the  pore  structure  may  attain $1.80~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g},$ the maximum  volume  recorded  for  porphyrin-comprising  MOFs. Due  to  this  morphological  characteristic,  the  PCN-600(Fe)  is considered extraordinarily stable in an aqueous medium with pH values ranging from 2 to 11. As far as our experience is concerned, it is the only highly porous porphyrinic MOF stable in aqueous environments.  PCN-600(Fe)  was  a  useful  POD  mimic  in accelerating co-oxidation [170].
Furthermore, Lian et al. discovered porous MOFs (PCN-888) with  three  distinct  pores.  The  largest  holes  have  diameters  of roughly $6.2\ \mathrm{nm}$ and  they  can  accept  a  single  glucose  oxidase molecule. Because the diameter of the intermediate pores is about $5.0\mathrm{nm}.$ only a single HRPs can be allowed to pass through. On the other hand, the small holes $(2.0\mathrm{nm})$ are not large enough to store biological molecules and only employed as a substrate migration pathway. Considering the exact binding order of the GOx and the HRP,  this  material  acted  as  a  tandem  nanoreactor.  These nanoreactors exhibit exceptional catalytic efficiency and negligible enzyme  leaching,  as  demonstrated  by  the  fact  that  catalytic performance  was  maintained  throughout  long  durations  of catalysis.  Furthermore,  the  PCN-888  structure  shielded  enclosed enzymes  against  trypsin  digestion,  indicating  the  nanoreactor’s resilience in vivo and in vitro [171].
## 3.3.2    Dimension
Since MOFs-based nanozymes are accessible in two dimensions, both 2D and 3D, the effect of the dimensions for nanozymes must be examined. Jin et al. used Co metal and 2-methylimidazole to synthesize a dodecahedral MOF (ZIF-67). The scanning electron microscopy  (SEM)  analysis  revealed  that  the  composites  of nanozymes have been homogenous dodecahedra with an average side  length  of  around $400~\mathrm{nm}$ .  Furthermore,  the  MOFs  have  a relatively  high  surface  area  of $1833.26\mathrm{~m}^{2}/\mathrm{g},$ allowing  them  to accept substrates efficiently and stimulate enzyme activities [172]. Feng  et  al.  have  synthesized  the  3D  MOF-like-heme  (PCN222(Fe)) employing Fe-TCPP and linker- $\mathrm{.}Z\mathrm{r}_{6}$ clustered as a node. It  has  the  most  recognized  1D  effective  channel  ( $3.7\ \mathrm{nm}$ in diameter), has POD-like performance, and is very stable [173]. Liu et  al.  reported  the  synthesis  of  POD-like  enzymes  for  PDT utilizing  ultrathin  2D  Cu-based  MOFs.  While  using  glucose oxidase, glucose may be repeatedly changed into gluconic acid and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ by avoiding using more concentrated and poisonous $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and  decreasing  the  undesirable  effects  [174].  The  electron diffraction  peaks  from  the  targeted  region  exhibited  reflections matching  the  (110)  and  (100)  planes,  confirming  its  smooth structure [175]. Gao et al. developed MOF Sm-TCPP nanoflakes. The nanosheets were $100~\mathrm{{nm}}$ in size and less than $10\ \mathrm{nm}$ thick. The  structural  features  improve  interconnections  of $\mathsf{S m}$ nodes with the material, leading to improved catalase-like performance [176]. Conversely, Yuan et al. claimed that using 2D materials in nanozymes  can  improve  their  catalytic  properties  due  to  their abundance  of  binding  sites.  However,  the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ reduction  and TMB oxidation efficiencies of their so-called 2D FeBTC nanozyme were  1.5–37.9  and 2.7–72.3  times  those  of  the  comparable  3D MOFs  [177].  Zhang  et  al.  investigated  a  ZIF-67-type  of  MOF nanostructures as another example. The MOF comprises multiple active sites and has higher catalytic oxidase-like performance due to  its  uniform  structure,  large  specific  surface  area,  and  tunable pore [178].
## 3.3.3    Morphology
The  reduced  surface-to-volume  ratio  of  3D  MOFs  confines  the adsorption  at  the  surface,  as  compared  to  2D  MOFs-based nanozymes; in the catalytic reaction, surface adsorption takes place in the first step. In addition, only a few active sites are shown on the surface, but many of them are buried within the framework due to generating the huge diffusion barrier. However, their less spreading in water solution almost confines the availability of the target  substance  to  active  sites,  which  also  delays  the  substrate diffusion process [179]. As a result, modifying the size or shape of MOF-based nanozymes to obtain a huge surface area, high surfaceto-volume  atomic  ratios,  and  a  small  diffusion  barrier  could significantly increase the performance [108, 180]. Cheng et al. [47] discovered  that  2D  Zn-TCPP(Fe)  has  a  greater  kinetic  rate  and two times greater catalytic activity than 3D Zn-TCPP(Fe). Wang et  al.  [180]  also  found  that  2D  MOFs  have  higher  enzymatic activity.  To  develop  an  effective  photocatalyst  for $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and artemisinin  synthesis,  they  linked  the  photosensitizer  porphyrin linker to the ZrBTB (BTB $\%$ 1,3,5-tris (4-carboxyphenyl) benzene) by strong $Z\mathrm{r}$ –carboxylate interaction. PCN-134-3D and PCN-134- 2D  are  produced  correspondingly  using  the  one-pot  technique and stepwise approach. Surprisingly, PCN-134-2D outperforms all previously  reported  homo-  or  heterogeneous  artemisinin photocatalysts, including 3D analogs, regarding artemisinin yield, which  could  be  associated  with  the  high  dispersibility  of  the individual nanosheets, more easily accessible catalytic centres, and facile substrate diffusion.
Furthermore, PCN-134-2D demonstrates smaller particles and a  more  steady  distribution,  leading  to  activity  augmentation. However,  modifications  in  the  surface  charge  of  MOFs  can modify  substrate  adsorption  and  boost  catalytic  performance. Wang et al. [181] used a solvothermal method to fabricate a sub$100~\mathrm{{nm}}$ POD-mimicking $Z\mathbf{r}$ -porphyrin  MOF  ( $\mathrm{\Sigmam}$ -PorMOF). Nevertheless,  as  its  reduced  size,  the  colloid $Z\mathrm{r}$ -PorMOF demonstrated  improved  stability  and  solubility  of  water,  which benefits the improvement of catalytic performance. Modifying 3D MOFs into 2D improves their imitating performance. It has been observed that the geometry of MOFs is also an essential element impacting  the  performance  of  mass  transport.  Pan  et  al.  [182] prepared  a  ficin@MOF  composite  by  immobilizing  fiction  in saucer-like zeolitic imidazolate structures, demonstrating 2.5-fold greater catalytic performance than the bulk crystalline MOFs. It is owing to the enormous surface area of the saucer-shaped MOF, which promotes substrate attraction and decreases mass transfer blockage.  It  demonstrated  that  size  reduction  and  morphology modification could enhance the catalytic performance.
## 3.3.4    Modification
Modifying the MOF-based nanozymes was an efficient method of regulating  their  performance  and  increasing  their  usefulness without affecting the MOF’s morphology [34]. The most frequent way to accomplish this includes metals. For instance, the MOFs containing  both  Zn  NPs  and  Ag  NPs  were  described  to  exhibit
POD-like activity by Kong and collaborators. They have also used it  for  fabricating  photochemical  biosensors  with  high  sensitivity. The nanozyme’s additional constituents catalyze the degradation of $^{3,3^{\prime}}$ -diaminobenzidine  to  produce  a  precipitate  with  a  lower photocurrent,  resulting  in  sensing  with  a  less  detection  speed [183].  Likewise,  Zhang  et  al.  developed  an  AuPt/ZIF-8-reduced graphene oxide (rGO) composite wherein highly distributed Au NPs  were  coupled  by  rGO.  Introducing  rGO  and  Au  NPs improved  the  enzymatic  performance  of  nanozyme  [184].  Ling et  al.  placed  ultra-small  Pt  NPs  on  the  MOFs  composed  of  Feporphyrin and $\mathrm{Zr^{4+}}$ (PMOF(Fe)); in another example, the MOFs serve  as  carriers  with  large  specific  surface  areas  with  open  Fe reactive regions in this material.
Furthermore, because of the bonding between the Pt NPs and MOFs, the Pt NPs are well disseminated, providing higher activity like  POD,  and  are  catalytically  effective  and  highly  stable. $\mathrm{Pt}@\mathrm{PMOF}(\mathrm{Fe})$ exhibits better catalase and POD-like functions to the distinct elements due to the synergistic action of PMOF(Fe) and  Pt  NPs  [185].  Hassan  et  al.  proposed  an  approach  for  the dispersion of ultra-small Ce NPs in $\mathrm{Ce}$ -MOF. Due to the efficient distribution  of  particularly  active  ultra-small  Ce  NPs,  the improved  MOF  demonstrated  excellent  electrocatalytic  POD activity  compared  to  the  initial  Ce-MOF.  While  at  a  similar moment,  the  existence  of  heterogeneous  oxidation  states  (i.e., $\mathrm{Ce^{3+}/C e^{4+}})$ resulted  in  enhancing  in  the  quantity  of  oxygencontaining functional groups, which was demonstrated by bands at 250, 540, 608, and $1171~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ in the Raman spectra of the Ce NPs.  These  gaps  serve  as  active  sites  for  the  catalytic  process, considerably improving the nanozyme’s catalytic activity [186].
Furthermore,  Liu  et  al.  studied  the  impact  of  adjusting  the weight  ratio  of  ZIF-67  and $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}$ to  make  raisin-like nanospheres $\mathrm{(ZIF{-}67/C u_{0.76}C o_{2.24}O_{4})}$ . ZIF-67 serves as a template and a cobalt source for the surface preparation of Co-Cu mixed oxides. The nanomaterial’s distinctive structure allows it to imitate the  action  of  four  enzymes,  glutathione  peroxidase,  dismutase, POD,  SOD,  and  laccase  [187].  ZIFs  offer  several  rewards,  for instance,  low  price  and  ease  of  fabrication.  However,  they disintegrate quickly in acidic environments. Hence, to increase the stability,  Zhao  et  al.  anticipated  the  bonding  of  ZIF-8  and  Fe polydopamine [188].
## 3.3.5    Surface modification of MOF-based nanozymes
The  modifying  surface  is  a  concise  process  for  changing  the characteristics of nanozymes, including water solubility, dispersibility, and charge properties, essential to catalytic reaction for  synthesizing  by  using  substantial  electrophilic  aromatic substitution. Liu et al. [174] treated the Fe-MOF termed porphyrinbased porous organic polymers (PPOPs) with chlorosulfonic acid to  form $\mathrm{FePPOPs–SO_{3}H}$ by  adding  sulfonic  acid  substituents, which improved the water solubilities of Fe-MOFs. Furthermore, FePPOPs- $\mathrm{SO_{3}H}$ retained $90.1\%$ activity even after five cycles and could  be  stored  at  ambient  temperature  for  20  days  without substantial  performance  reduction,  which  is  critical  for  practical use. Simultaneously, it was investigated that catalytic activity is also improved whenever the interaction between substrates and mimic enzymes  strengthens.  In  this  study,  the  surface  charge  turns negative,  causing $\mathrm{FePPOPs–SO_{3}H}$ to  produce  higher  POD-like activity [189]. Likewise, Song et al. [190] examined the influence of ssDNA  on  MIL-53  inherent  POD-like  characteristics  (Fe).  The study found that lengthy ssDNAs boost the enzyme activities of MIL-53(Fe),  which  might  be  attributed  to  the  higher  negatively charged  spots  following  DNA  modification.  Other  negatively charged compounds can be utilized to modify the surface of MOF. Dong  et  al.  [191]  devised  simple  post-synthetic  methods  for producing  glycine-MIL-53(Fe)  by  combining  MIL-53(Fe)  with glycine.
Reported  findings  exhibited  the  catalytic  performance  of modified  glycine-MIL-53(Fe),  greater  than  the  MIL-53(Fe). However,  the  geometry  and  crystalline  structure  of  MIL-53 remain  constant.  After  surface  modifications,  it  was  also demonstrated  that  a  reduction  in  the  positive  charge  on  the surface  of  the  nanozymes  increases  the  potential.  Furthermore, Tan  et  al.  [115, 192]  showed  that  the  improvement  of  TMB oxidation is caused by adsorbed negative ions on the surface of MOFs  rather  than  the  simple  addition  of  molecules  containing negative  ions  like  phosphate.  Overall,  increased  catalytic performance  may  be  acquired  by  surface  modification  for resulting  MOFs-based  nanozymes  with  substantially  unlike charges  on  the  substrates.  A  larger  surface  area  increases  the potential of catalytic sites, enhancing catalytic activity. The MOFs and  other  nanomaterials  with  a  large  surface  area  might  be reached  at  high  activity.  Ruan  et  al.  [48]  effectively  bonded  FeMOF to a large surface area, graphene oxide (GO). GO/Fe-MOF has  higher  water  solubility,  improved  POD  mimicking  activity, and reduced $K_{\mathrm{m}}$ value because of more active sites accessible to reactive  agents  than  Fe-MOF.  However,  encapsulating  the  NPs with the same enzyme-like behavior as MOFs to modify MOF is another  attractive  approach  to  enhance  active  catalytic  sites  and improve  catalytic  efficiency.  Although  Au  NPs  mimic  activities, the  inherent  disadvantages,  such  as  facile  aggregation  and  low catalytic  efficiency,  have  limited  their  independent  utilization. Zhang  et  al.  [129]  developed  a  nanocomposite $\mathrm{\u@NH_{2}}$ -MIL125(Ti) with POD-like behavior by distributing the Au NPs on the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti), which has exhibited more superior POD-like performance  and  greater  substrate  bindings  as  compared  to  the single $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti), and Au NPs.
## 4    Biosensing applications of MOFs-based nanozymes
## 4.1    Sensing of small biomolecules
The  MOF-based  nanozymes  biosensor  detects  the  targeted molecule, such as glucose. Glucose testing is mandatory for sugar level measurement, diabetes treatment, and the diagnosis of many other diseases. Colorimetric monitoring is among the most basic testing  methods.  Xu  et  al.  synthesized  nanozyme  by  embedding glucose oxidase in a Fe-based MOFs carrier $(\mathrm{Fe-MIL-88B–NH_{2}})$ . They observed glucose levels by measuring the formation of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2},$ the  byproduct  of  glucose  oxidation  through  nanozymes.  TMB turns blue due to peroxides. Amazingly, the approach can detect glucose concentrations ranging from 1 to $500~\mathrm{M}$ and lower than 0.487  M.  The $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ produced  by  the  glucose-oxidase  catalyzed glucose oxidation with $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ was instantly oxidized in situ from FeMOF,  reducing  transport  impedance  and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ degradation. Compared  to  the  free  enzyme  method,  the  combined  enzyme method  for  glucose  sensing  demonstrated  good  temperature tolerance,  acid–base  adaptation,  and  renewability  [217].  Other al.  encapsulated  glucose  oxidase  and  Ni-Pd  NPs  on  ZIF-8 simultaneously; the nanocomposite preserved both the activity of the immobilized glucose oxidase and the POD-like capabilities of the Ni-Pd nanomaterials. A glucose oxidase@ZIF-8-based multienzyme  mimic,  a  colorimetric  biosensor,  was  built  due  to  rapid glucose identification and a cascade mechanism for clear glucose monitoring  in  a  single  phase  [219].  Furthermore,  Zhong  et  al. developed $\mathrm{GOx}@\mathrm{MOF}{-}545(\mathrm{Fe}),$ and  the  detection  limit  of detection (LOD) was just $0.28~\mathrm{mL}$ allowing for the high specificity of  glucose  detection.  The  amazingly  functionalized  enzyme  has high  stability.  Subsequently,  after  seven  days  of  preservation  at normal  temperature,  encapsulated  enzyme  maintained  its activities  up  to $92\%$ ,  and  characteristics  of  the  allowed  enzyme were only $40\%$ maintained in a similar environment [220]. The MOFs in nanozymes act as a catalyst and carriers for safeguarding enzymes.  Tang  et  al.  discovered  the  combination  of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and MIL-101(Fe).  This  nanozymes  can  be  effective  in  oxidizing glucose  and  avoid  further  oxidation.  The  nanozymes  have  high enzymatic activity and a sensing range of $5\mathrm{-}100\mathrm{{lM}}.$ , allowing them to quantify $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ in pharmaceutical cleaning agents and glucose in individual blood samples [145]. Further, the Zhang group utilized $\mathrm{Co}_{x}\mathrm{O}_{y}\mathrm{H}_{z}$ -encapsulated $\mathrm{ZIF-}67/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanotubes to obtain a sensing limit  of  0.1  and  a  detection  limit  of  0.03.  The  sensor’s  broad detection  range  is  uncommon  and  might  be  employed  at  high glucose  concentrations  [221].  Nanozymes  were  effective  for fluorescence sensing and conventional colorimetric identification [222].  The  detecting  method  relies  on  the  nanozyme’s  glucoseoxidase activities, which convert glucose to $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2},$ causing a color change  or  fluorescence  effect.  As  a  result,  variations  in  light absorption can be used to detect glucose. Lin et al. found the MOFbased  nanozyme  MIL-53(Fe),  which  catalyzes  the  oxidation  of glucose and the reaction of its product $\mathrm{(H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2})$ with a fluorescent substrate,  terephthalic  acid  (TA).  Hence,  such  an  enzyme-based framework  can  produce  fluorescence.  This  characteristic  proved the  rapid  identification  of  glucose.  Furthermore,  Chen  et  al. demonstrated that the ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ encapsulated nanoscale MOF (nMOF) based  sensor  for  glucose  sensing.  Chemiluminescence  was obtained  near  luminol  and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ using  the  MOF’s  POD-like activity. Additionally, the chemiluminescence performance was up to $35\%$ [223].  These  examples  show  that $\operatorname{MOF}@$ -enzymes, immobilized  MOFs,  and  MOF-based  nanozymes  have  many applications in biosensing. MOFs-based nanozymes may similarly be utilized for the recognition of other organic compounds. For instance,  Badoei–Dalfard  et  al.  employed  thorium-based  MOF (Th-MOF) as uricase-like nanozyme. Attributed to the Th-MOF nanozyme, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ has been produced through the oxidation of uric acid,  and  measured $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ was  utilized  to  quantify  the  uric  acid contents [224]. Furthermore, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ can be an intermediary in the doped oxidation process. The advancement of colorimetric logic has pushed the adoption of nanozymes for sensing; for instance, Wang et al. proposed a novel method for identifying dopamine. They discovered that the $\mathrm{cu}$ (PDA)-(DMF) MOF might catalyze the oxidation of TMB in hydrogen peroxide, obtaining the blue color. Although dopamine suppresses the POD performance. As a result,  they  discovered  a  linkage  between  substrate  color  change and dopamine concentration [225]. Colorimetric techniques could also identify ascorbic acid [51] and salicylic acid [226]. Moreover, Tan’s group benefits from the fact that Cu-based MOFs may serve as  catalases  and  oxidase  nonfluorescent  ammonium  sulfate  to fluorescent  sulphur  in  the  existence  of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ .  They  used  these MOFs  for  the  monitoring  of  thiamine  [204].  In  another  case,
Zhang and colleagues discovered that by adding Au to the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ - MIL-125(Ti).  MOFs  serve  as  an  oxidase  capable  of  converting oxygen molecules to oxygen radicals. Furthermore, it was as lower as $0.026~\mathrm{nM}$ , with a maximum rate $(V_{\mathrm{max}})$ of $13.56\mathrm{nM}/\mathrm{s},$ and the obtained  results  are  considerably  better  than  natural  POD. Additionally, due to the Schottky junction between MOF and Au NPs,  the  reactive  molecules  are  influenced  by  light,  so  this molecule  may  be  utilized  for  detecting  the  photosensitive molecules (hemicystine). The nanozyme detects hemicystine from 1 to $10~\mathrm{mL}$ , with the smallest identification range of $0.15\mathrm{mL}$ [130]. Furthermore, Valekar et al. developed a new amine-grafted MOF with  enzymatic  activity  to  detect  acetylcholine  fluorescence  and choline. Their MOF, TMBDA-MIL-100(Fe), showed corresponding  choline  and  acetylcholine  minimum  detection values of 0.027 and 0.036 lM [227]. Further surprising, Guo et al. used the MIL-101(Fe) MOF, which has a POD-like function and exhibits  fluorescence  emission,  to  monitor  acetylcholine  and choline. The sensing device is label-free as well as very sensitive, with  detection  range  of  20.0  and $8.0\ \mathrm{nM}$ for  acetylcholine  and choline, correspondingly. Furthermore, the sensitivity was greater than  the  prior  studies  [228].  Hassanzadeh  et  al.  synthesized  a hybrid consisting of cholesterol oxidase and MOFs. Whereas the porous morphology of the MOF effectively adsorbs the cholesterol oxidase, leading to extremely higher enzymatic activity, and can monitor cholesterol contents ranging from 0.06 to $15~\mathrm{mM}$ with a $0.03~\mathrm{mM}$ limit  of  detection  [229].  Wang  et  al.  first  developed  a colorimetric assay for sensing harmful $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ microorganisms. CuMOF nanostructures accelerate the interaction of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ with TMB to form a yellow material. Furthermore, the amino molecules on the  surface  of  the  MOFs  enable  it  preferentially  to  adsorb Staphylococcus  aureus,  and  a  linear  association  between  the quantity  of S.  aureus and  the  absorbance  has  been  found. Moreover,  the  Cu-based  MOF  significantly  improved  catalytic performance.  This  approach  is  selective,  easy  and  has  a  high sensitivity, and is utilized to identify S. aureus at concentrations ranging  from  50  to $10,000\mathrm{\CFU/mL}$ with  limit  of  detection  of 20  CFU/mL  [230].  Cheng  et  al.  also  employed  Fe-MOF nanostructures to detect Salmonella enteritidis. This colorimetric immunoassay  showed  a  minimal  identification  value  of 34 CFU/mL and recovery rate of $94.68\%-124\%$ in milk, indicating Salmonella  enteritidis identification  in  actual  food  samples.  This study  is  a  valuable  resource  for  detecting  foodborne  infections [231].
## 4.2    Detection of heavy metal ions
MOF-based nanozymes can detect metal ions in an environment and microorganisms. Li et al. developed the $\mathrm{Pt}\mathrm{NPs@UiO-}66\mathrm{-}\mathrm{NH_{2}}$ nanozyme by doping MOFs with Pt NPs. This nanozyme mimics POD  but  can  adsorb  mercury  ions $\mathrm{(Hg^{+})}$ ,  resulting  in  a  loss  of capacity  to  catalyze  the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ degradation  process.  Nevertheless, this means that it can detect $\mathrm{Hg^{+}}$ ions. Moreover, approximately $99\%$ of $\mathrm{Hg^{+}}$ was recovered from water [128].
Furthermore, Wang et al. presented a technique to determine the $\mathrm{Hg^{+}}$ levels in atmospheric water samples. Modified valence Cebased MOFs were found to have oxidase-like activity due to mixed valence $\mathrm{Ce}$ ions. Furthermore, single-stranded DNA can attach to the MOFs, inhibit active sites, and reduce its enzymatic activity. Wang  et  al.  used  the  capability  of $\mathrm{Hg^{+}}$ to  interact  with  singlestranded  DNA  to  produce  double-stranded  DNA  to  develop  a colorimetric  approach  for $\mathrm{Hg^{+}}$ ion  determination.  Synthesized sensors could detect $\mathrm{Hg^{+}}$ contents ranging from 0.05 to $6\mathrm{M}_{;}$ , with the lowest detection value of $10.5~\mathrm{nM}.$ . The sensor demonstrated outstanding  sensitivity  for $\mathrm{Hg^{+}}$ identification  [232].  Li  et  al. fabricated  a  ZIF/GO  hybrid  nanozyme  that  catalyzes  the interaction  of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and $\mathbf{Ag}^{+}$ ,  allowing  for  precise  and  sensitive identification  of $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{g}^{+}$ in  human  serum  and  water  samples. Moreover, the detection limit of $\mathbf{Ag}^{+}$ ions nanomaterial is $1.43\mathrm{nM},$ , significantly  lower  than  the  0.93  M  threshold  achieved  for previous nanozyme-based colorimetric approaches [233].
## 4.3    Detection of biomacromolecules
Another  important  use  of  MOF-based  nanozymes  is  the identification  of  proteins,  nucleic  acids,  and  other  biological macromolecules.  For  instance,  Wang  et  al.  reported  the detestation of phosphorylated proteins using a $Z\mathrm{r}$ -based MOF. In $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2},$ the bipyridine ligand-containing MOFs catalyze the enzyme that converts TMB between its colorless and colorful states. In the presence of a protein, the couplings between the phosphate groups of  the  proteins  and $Z\mathrm{r}$ in  the  nanozyme  resulted  in  nanozyme inactivation; hence, the phosphorylated protein may be recognized by accompanying chronic processes [234]. Ruan et al. described a nanozyme  consisting  of  Fe-MOF  and  GO.  This  nanozyme  can identify  biomarkers  of  wood  smoke  exposure  in  blood  serum. Because  of  its  huge  surface  area  and  numerous  active functionalities, nanozyme demonstrated good POD-like performance  and  has  a  limit  of  detection  is $0.268\mathrm{ng/mL},$ exhibiting  high  sensitivity  [48].  Furthermore,  Wang  et  al. fabricated  a  simple,  label-free  colorimetric  approach  for biomolecule  identification  using  Fe-based  MOFs  (Fe-MIL-88A). Their MOF-aptamer-modified technology is innovative and able to  monitor  a  range  of  species  in  blood  samples  of  humans (biomolecules), including thrombin. The nanocomposite material does  have  a  good  selectivity  for  thrombin  (10  times  more  than other  disrupting  reagents)  [235].  Several  detecting  approaches, including  fluorescence,  were  also  performed  to  evaluate biomacromolecule-containing  materials.  Li  et  al.  suggested  a fluorescent  detection  approach  for  acid  phosphatase  employing $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-101 MOF as both a catalyst and fluorescent signal. The difference of two luminescent signals recorded in the existence of MOFs, acid phosphatase, and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2},$ is produced in this biosensor, enabling acid phosphatase contents to be measured. In particular, $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-101  has  an  intrinsic  fluorescence  property  at $456\ \mathrm{nm}$ (F456). It catalyzed the oxidation of phenylenediamine (OPD) by $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ to  produce  luminous  2,3-diamino  phenazine  with  the highest  emission  at $556\ \mathrm{nm}$ as  a  POD-like  nanozyme  (F556).
When PPi and ACP were added to the $\mathrm{NH_{2}{-}M I L{-}}101/\mathrm{OPD}/\mathrm{H_{2}O_{2}}$ structure,  the  ratio-metric  luminescence  signal  (F556/F456)  and the ratio-metric fluorescence sensing for the rapid identification of PPi  and  evaluating  acid  enzymatic  activities  may  be  generated. The  sensor  might  monitor  acid  phosphatase  at  values  ranging from  0.01  to ${}^{30}\ \mathrm{U/L},$ with  the  smallest  recognition  range  being $0.005\ \mathrm{\U/L}$ .  Unexpectedly,  this  nanozyme  might  be  applied  to identify concentrations of acid phosphatase in serum [236]. The doped  MOFs  have  such  a  high  recognition  ability  as  well.  This material’s  framework  and  metallic  modification  resulted  in extremely  active  regions  and  good  oxidase-like  performance. Acetylcholinesterase  could  have  been  measured  from  0.0001  to $1\ \mathrm{mU/mL}$ with  a  detection  limit  of $0.1\ \mathrm{mU/mL}$ by  using  this sensor. Furthermore, the material had a minimal pH dependency and was biodegradable [237]. Additionally, MOF-based nanozymes can monitor enzymes, including phosphatase [96, 112] and  deoxyribonuclease  [190].  All  of  the  techniques  discussed above are colorimetric and fluorescence-based. However, electrical signal  monitoring  is  indeed  common.  Li  et  al.  discovered  a technique  to  monitor  exosomal  micro-RNA  (miRNA),  crucial tumor  biomarkers.  They  combined  the  target  chemical  for identification  with  a  multi-layered  nanozyme  material  using  a cascade primer exchange reaction (MIL-88@Pt@MIL-88). $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ is degraded in this system, resulting in an amplified electric signal, and  the  linear  connection  between  the  content  of  the  target chemical  and  the  electrical  signal  was  observed.  The  sensor demonstrated high selectivity for the targeted miRNA [238]. Sun and colleagues devised a technique for detecting cardiac troponin I, a biomarker for myocardial infarction. To adsorb the marker, a dual-probe  linked  to  a  DNA  nano  tetrahedron  was  employed, which was then joined with a magnetic nanozyme modified with $\operatorname{Cu@Au}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ $(\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}@U I O-66/C u@A u})$ to produce a super sandwich-type structure. Simultaneously, the MOF-based nanozyme catalyzes the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ -assisted oxidation of hydroquinone to  benzoquinone  (BQ),  resulting  in  electric  signal  that  allows cardiac  troponin  I  identification.  The  material  demonstrated strong catalytic activity due to the characteristics of both $\mathrm{Cu@Au}$ and  UIO-66  as  nanozymes.  As  a  result,  it  has  a  very  broad detection  range $(0.05{-}100~\mathrm{\ng/mL})$ and  an  extremely  lower detection  limit  of $16\ \mathrm{pg/mL}$ [239].  Protein  identification  using nanozymes  is  conceivable,  as  previously  demonstrated.  They synthesized a nanozyme modified with Pt NPs, Pt@P-MOF(Fe), that  served  as  the  basic  material  for  the  sensor.  This  nanozyme oxidizes  and  serves  as  a  carrier  for  an  additional  DNA  strand (aDNA1).  When  telomerase  is  added  to  the  sensing  system,  a strand of helper DNA2 is stretched, yielding hairpin DNA. The liberated  DNA  hybridizes  with  DNA1  on  the  interface  of  the MOF, and the telomerase concentration may be measured using the  electrical  signal  produced  by  this  nanozyme’s  reduction  of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}.$ . Amazingly, the sensor may also be utilized to quantify HeLa cell  concentration  [240].  The  biomacromolecules  are  essential ingredient  for  life,  hence,  reliable  techniques  for  determining biomacromolecules are important in the life sciences, particularly medical diagnostics. The instances in this article demonstrate the wide  range  of  applications  and  high  potential  of  MOF-based nanozymes.  In  addition  to  the  experiments  presented  here, numerous  others  have  demonstrated  the  effectiveness  of  MOFbased nanozymes for biosensing of biomolecules [241–245].
## 4.4    Detection of amino acids and drugs
MOFs-based electrochemical sensor was used to detect drugs. The bare electrode was then treated with Au@ZIF-67 before being electropolymerized  on  the  Au@ZIF-67  changed  surface  of  the electrode.  Eventually,  the  MIP  was  created  using  the  cyclic voltammetry (CV) approach. The synthesized sensor can identify Tyr through $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking binding and hydrogen interactions. As a built-in probe, the sensor displayed high sensing capacity with a detection limit of $79~\mathrm{nM}$ in a dynamic range of $0.1~\mathrm{{nM}}$ to $4\upmu\mathrm{M}.$ Feng et al. [256] prepared carbon fibre/ZIF-67 nanocomposite and proved  the  influence  of  carbon  fibre  structure  on  ZIF-67 enzymatic  performance.  PCF  prevents  ZIF-67  nanocrystal aggregating  and  growth,  producing  a  ZIF-67/PCF  hybrid  with more  binding  regions,  improved  conductance,  and  greater transport  channels.  In  the  measurement  of  L-cysteine,  the produced composites have shown strong electrocatalytic response in the ranges of 5–160 and $160{-}1580\mathrm{M}$ with a detection limit of $0.5~\mathrm{M}.$ . Peng et al. [257] employed MIL-101(Fe) functionalized by Ag  NPs  to  determine  tryptophan $({\mathrm{Trp}})$ .  The  manufactured  Ag NPs/MIL-101/GCE showed a favorable reaction to $\mathrm{Trp}$ oxidation with  a  low  detection  limit  of $0.14~\mathrm{M}.$ Wu  et  al.  [258]  recently developed  an  electrochemical  biosensor  based  on  Cu-MOF  for detecting  L-tyrosine  (L-Tyr)  in  biomimetic  environments.  As  a MOF material, Cu offers naked reactive sites and well-organized frameworks that may selectively detect the small biomolecule LTyr. In the range of concentrations of 0.01 to $0.09~\mathrm{mM};$ , this CuMOF electrochemical biosensor showed high sensitivity and ease of operation for L-Tyr. 5.822 M was determined as a LOD.
## 5 Conclusions and perspectives
MOFs-based  nanozymes  are  gaining  popularity  due  to  their distinct  properties.  MOF-based  nanozymes  combine  the  flexible features  of  MOF  materials  with  attractive  enzyme-like  potential and  have  explored  applications  ranging  from  monitoring  and biomedical  to  environmental  science  and  catalytic  production. MOF-based  nanozymes  are  used  to  develop  many  rules  and methodologies  for  determining  analytes,  particularly  in  the biochemical  analytical  field.  In  this  review,  we  summarized  the recent  advances  of  different  2D  and  3D  MOF-based  composite nanozymes  and  their  applications  in  biosensing.  We  first categorized  MOF-based  nanozymes.  Then,  the  distinctive applications  of  MOF-based  nanozymes  in  identifying  various analytes are thoroughly studied.
Despite  state-of-the-art  development,  manufacturing,  and research performance, MOF-based nanozymes used in biochemical  monitoring  remain  in  their  beginning.  Some difficulties  should  be  considered  for  their  future  growth  in  this area.  (1)  The  synthesis  of  MOF-based  nanozymes  with  the required efficiency. As previously stated, because of their relatively larger  size  and  restricted  active  sites,  recently  produced  MOFbased  nanozymes  generally  demonstrate  unsatisfactory  catalytic performance. While various solutions for improving the intrinsic activity  of  MOF-based  nanozymes  were  developed  and  proved. Consequently, their catalytic activity was superior as compared to natural  enzymes.  Effective  methods  of  drastically  increasing  the low  activity  of  MOF-based  nanozymes  are  necessary  [44, 262, 263]. (2) Stability of MOF in an aqueous medium is another task for MOF materials utilized as nanozymes. The bulk of MOFs is currently  produced  in  organic  solvents.  Their  stability  in  an aqueous  solution  should  be  considered  when  using  them  as nanozymes.  Nanozymes  must  be  reusable  and  recyclable.  The present  analytical  investigations  do  not  evaluate  the  reusable performance  of  MOF-based  nanozymes.  A  stable  MOF-based nanozymes  in  an  aqueous  medium  would  enhance  their reusability and sensing application. (3) Research on multifunctional  MOFs-based  nanozymes.  MOF  materials  with metal  nodes  and  organic  ligands  can  display  various characteristics,  including  enzyme-like  behavior.  Integrating enzyme-mimicking catalytic activity and other functionalities into a single MOF material is better for future development since such a multifunctional MOF will find many fascinating applications in many disciplines [43, 55]. Nevertheless, there are still a few hurdles to solve, such as how to guarantee the numerous activities of MOFbased  nanozyme  operate  effectively  without  mutual  interference and enable all functions to serve synergistically for a specified goal. Aside  from  luminous  MOF-based  nanozymes,  it  is  widely anticipated  that  unique  MOF-based  nanozymes  with  fascinating magnetic,  thermal,  and  electrochemical  characteristics  will  be developed [264]. (4) Using MOF-based nanozymes to investigate novel  sensing  principles  and  techniques.  Although  various nanozyme-based  monitoring  methods  for  specific  analytes  have been developed, the kind and quantity of targets that nanozymes can  analyze  remain  extremely  limited  [265].  The  catalytic properties of nanozymes should improve signals, which have been anticipated in ELISA, electrochemical, and biosensors. They were applied  to  environmental  detection,  food  safety  measures, biomarker sensing, and diagnosis of disorders. Developing fresh ideas  and  methodologies  and  combining  nanozymes  with  other sophisticated  biological  identification  methods  would  be  a  hot topic in the coming decades. (5) Specific identification. One of the most significant challenges to using nanozymes in biosensing is a shortage of substrate selectivity [266]. Detecting analytes selectively via  nanozyme  catalysis  depends  on  unique  interactions  between analytes  and  nanozymes  or  bio-recognition  components (aptamers and antibodies). Furthermore, enhancing the catalytic selectivity  of  nanozymes  using  suitable  ways  (e.g.,  non-natural molecular imprinting) is a broad approach for achieving specific identification  [267].  It  is  well  understood  that  their  particular spatial  configurations  mostly  determine  the  great  catalytic specificity  of  natural  enzymes.  The  enzyme-like  catalytic properties  of  MOFs  and  their  versatility  are  gaining  increasing interest in the research community. Furthermore, the low activity of MOF-based nanozymes would be rectified using sophisticated nanotechnology  and  molecular  manufacturing.  In  the  coming decades,  several  kinds  of  multifunctional  MOF-based  nanozyme will be exposed. These advancements would considerably increase its application in different research areas, particularly biosensing. (6) Although the exerted active functional groups or active sites make  the  MOFs  as  independent  nanozyme  catalysts  with  great potential analytical applications (such as MIL MOFs), their activity is far lower than that of the natural enzyme, probably due to the relatively low reactivity of pristine MOFs. Thus, the design of new MOFs-based highly active nanozymes is urgently needed. Using the MOFs as hosts to fabricate the MOFs-based composites is a promising strategy to obtain highly functional nanozymes because of  the  confinement  effects  of  MOFs,  in  which  the  MOFs encapsulated/immobilized metal NPs or the MOFs encapsulated natural  enzymes  and  nanozymes  received  great  interest  and became  a  hot  topic  in  biosensing.  However,  the  interactions between  MOFs  and  the  encapsulated  metal  NPs  or  metal composite  nanomaterial  or  enzymes  (including  natural  enzymes and  nanozymes)  remain  to  be  explored.  Another  challenge  in nanozyme-based  nanotechnology  is  the  utilization  of  multi functionalities  in  one  composite  material.  For  example,  the controllable integration of optically active materials within MOFs is  a  promising  strategy  to  endow  the  MOFs  with  synergistic catalytic  effects  and  some  additional  properties,  like  the  SERS activities,  when  integrating  Au  NPs  and  Ag  NPs  into  MOF nanozymes.  This  inaugurates  new  avenues  for  the  design  of multifunctional  nanozymes  with  other  materials  and  expands their biosensing applications.
